From: Manuel de Brito Fontes <aledbf@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 12:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] x86/apic/msi: Skip the message write when the affinity change
 moves nothing

request_irq() of a PCI MSI-X interrupt writes its message twice in a
row. Activation assigns the vector and msi_domain_activate() writes the
message; irq_startup() then sets the affinity before the entry is
unmasked (IRQCHIP_AFFINITY_PRE_STARTUP), the vector domain keeps the
vector because its CPU is in the mask (assign_vector_locked() and
assign_managed_vector() return early), and msi_set_affinity() writes
the same message again. For an MSI-X entry that is three writes to the
table and a read to flush them, each an exit to the VMM in a guest.

Return from msi_set_affinity() without writing when neither the vector
nor the destination APIC ID changed. The message is composed from those
two alone (__irq_msi_compose_msg()), and the device already holds it:
apic_set_affinity() refuses an interrupt that is not activated, and the
vector and destination of an activated interrupt change only through
activation and through this function, both of which write the message.
A device whose table was lost to a reset or a sleep state gets it back
from pci_restore_msi_state(), which writes the message the descriptor
recorded, the same one. An affinity change that moves the interrupt
writes as before, through the same paths.

Counted from the source for this machine's six virtio devices, 19
vectors, each requested at probe: 19 writes of four MMIO accesses to
the MSI-X table BAR, 76 exits by the first login, of which 56 were measured
gone. Nested on the bench fleet an exit costs ~6.7 us, about 0.4 ms.

The one behaviour that changes is for a device that loses its MSI-X
table without the PCI core restoring it: an affinity change to the CPU
it already targets no longer happens to repair the entry.

Measured on the bench fleet (nested KVM, Xeon 6975P-C, one vCPU, the
workspace's shape), alone this patch moved the first SSH login by less
than the noise. With the other four patches that remove exits from
device setup (the IO-APIC table, the state save at add, virtio's
capabilities, queue_select, the MSI message), the cold first login was
9.4 ms faster, 95% interval [5.7, 14.2], over 120 interleaved boots a
side (2026-11-23), and a restored guest's login did not move.

Local patch for this machine. Not upstream.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 arch/x86/kernel/apic/msi.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/x86/kernel/apic/msi.c b/arch/x86/kernel/apic/msi.c
index 66bc5d3e7..7fb7c25f8 100644
--- a/arch/x86/kernel/apic/msi.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/apic/msi.c
@@ -48,6 +48,18 @@ msi_set_affinity(struct irq_data *irqd, const struct cpumask *mask, bool force)
 	if (ret < 0 || ret == IRQ_SET_MASK_OK_DONE)
 		return ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * The parent keeps the vector when its CPU is in the new mask, as
+	 * it is when the interrupt starts up. The device then holds this
+	 * message already: the vector and destination of an activated
+	 * interrupt change only through activation and through here, and
+	 * both write it. Writing it again only costs MMIO accesses to the
+	 * device, each a VM exit in a guest.
+	 */
+	if (cfg->vector == old_cfg.vector &&
+	    cfg->dest_apicid == old_cfg.dest_apicid)
+		return ret;
+
 	/*
 	 * For non-maskable and non-remapped MSI interrupts the migration
 	 * to a different destination CPU and a different vector has to be
